# Mi Pad 2 recovery tools

These local-console tools create and restore BTRFS Timeshift snapshots for the current Mi Pad 2 installation.

## Backup

Run `sudo mp2-backup`. It verifies `/` is BTRFS and `/boot` is a separate VFAT mount, detects the actual BTRFS root block device, archives `/boot`, then creates a manual Timeshift snapshot on that device. The archive and SHA-256 checksum are named for the exact snapshot, so an older root snapshot is never paired with an unrelated boot archive.

## Inspect and restore

`sudo mp2-recover --status` lists snapshots on the detected root device. `sudo mp2-recover` performs an interactive restore; `sudo mp2-recover --yes` is for deliberate non-interactive recovery.

Restore requires a matching per-snapshot `/boot` archive and valid checksum, plus `/boot` currently mounted as VFAT. Missing/corrupt archives, a non-BTRFS root, a changed `/boot` source, or an unknown root block device stop the restore before boot files are extracted. The utility does not mount partitions itself.

The scripts assume the installed system's root BTRFS device is the Timeshift snapshot device and that `/boot` is VFAT. Check `findmnt /` and `findmnt /boot` before relying on recovery. Old backups made by versions before the per-snapshot archive change are not auto-restorable by the new script; preserve them separately until migrated or no longer needed.

## Test without changing a system

Run `sudo sh fix_file/tests/test-mipad2-recovery.sh`. The test stubs `findmnt`, `mountpoint`, `timeshift`, `tar`, and `sync`, and verifies detected-device routing, archive/checksum matching, and fail-closed behavior. It does not mount or restore real filesystems.

The `Mi Pad 2 recovery safety` GitHub Actions workflow runs shell syntax checks and this mocked test when the recovery package or test changes.
